BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b8d14c610f0038954fe6"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Jill Lepore: The Artificial State
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T200000
UID:6a85b8d14c610f0038954fe6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL and Toronto Metropolitan University present Pulitzer-Prize
  winning historian Jill Lepore in conversation with Sanjay Ruparelia at th
 e Toronto Reference Library's Appel Salon.\nAs Professor of American Histo
 ry at Harvard University and Professor of Law at Harvard Law School\, Jill
  Lepore brings her rare gift for narrative history to one of the most urge
 nt questions of our time: has democracy already been quietly replaced by r
 ule of a different kind\, one governed not by citizens\, but by machines o
 wned by corporations?\nIn her new book\nThe Rise and Fall of the Artificia
 l State\n\, Lepore traces our increasing dependence on and strangulation b
 y data. Framing the book as a successor to Hannah Arendt's\nThe Origins of
  Totalitarianism\n\, Lepore shares a new history for our twenty-first cent
 ury.\nWhat does it mean when the era of the liberal nation-state seems to 
 be giving way to the rule of artificial intelligence? Lepore insists histo
 ry is driven not by machines but by individuals\, societies\, cultures\, a
 nd ideas\, and that the architects of the Artificial State drew their gove
 rning philosophy not from science\, but from science fiction.\nCan what ha
 s been built be undone? And what would it take to reimagine a genuinely de
 mocratic relationship between technology and self-governance\, one where h
 umans\, not machines\, remain accountable to one another?\nJoin one of Ame
 rica's most celebrated historians\, fresh from her bestselling\nWe the Peo
 ple: A History of the U.S. Constitution\n\, for an essential conversation 
 about power\, technology\, and the fight to reclaim democratic life from t
 he architects of an artificial future.\nQuestion and answer session and bo
 ok signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout this Event's Gu
 ests:\nJill Lepore\nSanjay Ruparelia\nRead More:\nRise and Fall of the Art
 ificial State\nWe the People\n***\nThis event is part of our\nOn Civil Soc
 iety\nseries\, where we host local and international authors\, activists a
 nd academics in conversation about the most critical topics of our time. G
 enerously supported by the McConnell Foundation and also by Chris M. Reid.
 \n***\nAbout the series:\nOn the Frontlines of Democracy\nis a public lect
 ure series to analyze the trajectory of democracy in the twenty-first cent
 ury.\nIn every region\, the prospects of democracy face serious challenges
 \, old and new. Can we protect constitutional rights and renew collective 
 self-rule in an era of popular mistrust\, severe partisanship and resurgen
 t nationalism? Can they reduce inequalities of power\, wealth and status\,
  defend deep diversity and confront climate change in the new digital age?
  Can we develop innovative strategies to revitalize civic engagement\, emp
 ower public institutions and resist autocratic threats? And what can we of
 fer\, learn and do to promote the prospects of democracy in our increasing
 ly post-western global order?\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT\n:\nLocation: Bram and
  Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd fl
 oor\nTime: Doors open 6:00 pm\, Event starts 7 pm.\nTickets: Free registra
 tion required.\nTicket policy: Tickets guaranteed until 6:50 pm\, after wh
 ich rush line admission begins. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar: B
 eer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\nTPL is committed to 
 accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to requ
 est accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.c
 a or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformati
 on Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected 
 by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20
 (a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and p
 rogram registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contact
 ed to fill out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about how
  the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff 
 at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G
 8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b8d14c610f0038954fe6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
