BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Civic Tech Toronto": "https://guild.host/events/civic-meetup-564-a-datadriven-w2fxru"}
X-SOURCE-ID:guildhost_civic_tech
SUMMARY:Civic Meetup #564: A Data-Driven Approach to Toronto's Electoral G
 eography
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T210000
UID:abce6f82a43b485ffeeb262649402740@guild.host
DESCRIPTION:Venue not yet confirmed. We're expecting to be at Myhal Centre
  (University of Toronto)\, but please check this page closer to the date f
 or the final location and room number.&#x20\;\n\nTopic: A Data-Driven Appr
 oach to Toronto's Electoral Geography\n\nWith colleagues\, Zach Taylor bui
 lt for research purposes fine-grained maps of historical election results 
 in Toronto and other cities. He will discuss how they built the maps\, wha
 t they have used them for\, and how spatial data like this could be used i
 n the future.\n\nSpeaker: Zack Taylor (he/him)\n\nZack Taylor is a profess
 or of Political Science and Geography at the University of Western Ontario
  where he specializes in urban politics\, local governance\, and political
  geography.\n\nAgenda:\n\n7:00-7:20 Welcome and Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50
  Presentation and Q\\&A/discussion\n\n7:50-9:00 Breakout groups\n\n‎\n\n
 Code of Conduct: https://civictech.ca/about-us\n\n‎\n\nCheck in with us 
 on the Civic Tech Toronto Slack: https://link.civictech.ca/chat\n\n‎\n\n
 About Us:\n\nOur weekly civic meetups bring together Torontonians (designe
 rs\, coders\, urban planners\, government staff\, mappers\, policy-makers\
 , students\, communications strategists\, community organizers\, and more)
  who share an interest in making Toronto more responsive\, prosperous\, su
 stainable\, and equitable through design\, tech\, and data.\n\nCome and be
  part of it!\n\nFor more info: https://civictech.ca\n\n\\--\n\nSuggest a S
 peaker:&#x20\;\n\nInterested in speaking at a future event\, or know someo
 ne who'd be a great speaker? We'd love to hear from you: https://link.civi
 ctech.ca/speaker-suggestions\n\nSuggest a Venue:&#x20\;\n\nHave a space th
 at could host up to 80 civic-minded folks for an evening? Consider recomme
 nding your venue: https://link.civictech.ca/venue-suggestions\n
LOCATION:Online: https://link.civictech.ca/zoom / Myhal Centre for Enginee
 ring Innovation and Entrepreneurship (MY)
URL:https://guild.host/events/civic-meetup-564-a-datadriven-w2fxru
X-SOURCE:Civic Tech Toronto
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Luma Toronto Discover": "https://luma.com/tf6s6ycw"}
X-SOURCE-ID:luma_toronto_discover
SUMMARY:Cutting The Fashion Body: Why The Fashion Image Is No Longer Still
  by Nathalie Khan
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T210000
UID:evt-lsCH62VBZHvkgX6@events.lu.ma
DESCRIPTION:Address:\nBeyond Space\nToronto\, ON\nCanada\n\nAn extra Octob
 er meeting\, bringing Fashion Book Club to the east end!\nWe'll be gatheri
 ng at La Maison Atelier\, the concept store of Toronto label Beyond Space\
 , to read Nathalie Khan's "Cutting the Fashion Body: Why the Fashion Image
  Is No Longer Still."\nExploring how fashion film has changed the way we s
 ee clothes and the body\, Khan's essay asks what means for fashion to exis
 t in motion. We'll screen three of the four fashion films Khan references\
 , and discuss how moving images shape the way we see and make fashion toda
 y.\nSee you there!\nPDF here\n\nHosted by Fashion Book Club & BEYOND SPACE
  INC.
LOCATION:Beyond Space\, 349 Jones Ave\, Toronto\, ON M4J 3G4\, Canada
URL:https://luma.com/tf6s6ycw
X-SOURCE:Luma Toronto Discover
