BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Engineering Engineering Events": "https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/engineering-leadership-navigating-a-changing-world/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:engineering_engineering_events
X-SOURCE:UofT Engineering
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T130000
DTSTAMP:20260925T161609
CREATED:20260821T202306Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260821T202850Z
UID:10004665-1791979200-1791982800@www.engineering.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Engineering leadership: Navigating a changing world
DESCRIPTION:Engineering has always addressed challenges by advancing technology. But success now depends not only on technical expertise but also on the ability to connect people\, foster collaboration and drive innovation across complex environments.  \nWhat does it take to lead as an engineer in an era defined by AI\, global collaboration and rapid change?\nJoin Professor Emily Moore for an engaging discussion on how engineering careers are evolving and how leaders can adapt to meet new challenges. Drawing on her research into engineering career paths\, teamwork and organizational dynamics\, Professor Moore will share practical insights on developing the skills that matter for long-term success. Discover how engineers can strengthen their leadership capabilities\, navigate change with confidence and amplify their impact in a world where the human side of engineering has never been more important.  \nJoin us on October 14\, 2026 at our monthly Skule™ Lunch & Learn.  \nBy registering for the Skule™ Lunch & Learn event\, you could potentially earn Continuing Professional Development (CPD) credits. CPDs are essential for professional engineers and limited license holders to renew their licenses annually through the PEO PEAK Program. If you’re wondering whether this event offering qualifies you for CPD credits\, click here to learn more. \nAbout the speaker\nProfessor Emily Moore was appointed Director of Troost Institute for Leadership Education in Engineering (ILead) in October 2018\, after more than twenty years in industry. Emily started her career at the Xerox Research Centre of Canada scaling up new materials and processes from the lab to manufacturing. In her 11 years at Xerox\, Emily learned a great deal about leading teams and developing new products\, becoming a manager and Principal Engineer. Emily then spent 10 years at Hatch\, a global engineering firm serving the mining\, energy and infrastructure sectors. Emily led international teams to develop new product and service offerings and to deliver major projects\, first as the Director of Technology Development and then as Managing Director\, Water. Emily was also the inaugural chair of Hatch’s Global Diversity and Inclusion efforts. Emily holds a bachelor’s degree in engineering chemistry from Queen’s University and completed a doctorate in physical chemistry from Oxford University as a Rhodes Scholar. In 2016 Emily was recognized as one of 100 Global Inspirational Women in Mining and received the SCI Canada Kalev Pugi Award. 
URL:https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/engineering-leadership-navigating-a-changing-world/
LOCATION:Online
CATEGORIES:Lectures, seminars and workshops
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"The AI Collective": "https://luma.com/aic-gl-10-14"}
X-SOURCE-ID:luma_genai_collective
SUMMARY:Can AI Help Prevent Toxic Relationships and Gender-Based Violence?
  Lessons from Finland and Brazil
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T133000
UID:evt-VqJLcNRFMluK5qx
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://images.lumacdn.com/uploads/9k/215d5d2f-3
 a31-43f8-8486-83d2c67d9172.png
DESCRIPTION:Hosted by: The AI Collective\, Ana De Alencar\, AJ Green\, Ana
  Catarina De Alencar\n\nTickets: Free\n\nInterested: 0
