SUMMARY:How Canadians are Feeling about Democracy These Days
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T193000
UID:6a99ae1baafa610029615271@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Trust in democratic institutions is being challenged in Canada
  and around the world. While many Canadians still believe in democratic va
 lues\, more people are worried about political divisions\, lower trust in 
 governments and public institutions\, false information\, and whether poli
 tical systems are meeting people's needs.\nUsing the latest public opinion
  polls\, election research\, and public engagement data\, this panel will 
 explore how Canadians feel about democracy today. Are concerns about democ
 racy shared by many Canadians\, or are they more common in certain groups?
  What is causing changes in political trust and participation? And what ca
 n governments\, community organizations\, educators\, and local communitie
 s do to help strengthen democracy and encourage people to get involved in 
 the future?\nFeaturing\nAndrew Parkin\n\, Executive Director\,\nEnvironics
  Institute\nSarah Morra\,\nChair\, Vote 16 Canada and Research Assistant\,
 \nThe Samara Centre for Democracy\nJoanna Massie\,\nPostdoctoral Fellow in
  Political Science\,\nMcMaster University\nModerator:\nShawn Micallef\n\, 
 Toronto Star columnist & University of Toronto civics instructor\nToronto'
 s municipal election takes place on October 26\, 2026. Check out the\nKnow
 YourVote.TO\nwebsite and the\nCelebrate Democracy\npage to learn more abou
 t the upcoming election\, access important voter resources\, and discover 
 activities and interactive library programs for all ages happening at a br
 anch near you.\nThis initiative is made possible by donations to the Toron
 to Public Library Foundation and is generously supported by the McConnell 
 Foundation.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on t
 his form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libra
 ries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only b
 e used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Quest
 ions about how the library handles your information can be directed to Ans
 werline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, 
 Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a99ae1baafa610029615271
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e24aafa61002961b09b"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:Vibe Coding: Make Your Own Interactive Story with AI
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T200000
UID:6a9b4e24aafa61002961b09b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What if you could write a story where the reader chooses what 
 happens\, like a branching adventure\, a mystery\, or a game\, and have AI
  build it with you?\nIn this class\, you'll learn "vibe-coding": using an 
 AI coding assistant to build your own interactive story or simple game. Yo
 u'll design the branches\, describe the rules in plain language\, test for
  the tricky edge cases (what if the player picks the weird option?)\, and 
 end up with a single file you can share with anyone. Just a story you want
  to tell. This is the playful sibling of the practical vibe-coding worksho
 p.\nPrerequisites: Mouse\, keyboarding and internet skills. Computers ar
 e provided. No coding experience required. If you wish to save your work
  from this hands-on program\, please bring your own USB stick.\nRegistrati
 on is required and will start September 9\, 2026 at 9:00 AM.\nNote: Your s
 pot will not be guaranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nA
 fter this time\, remaining seats will be filled on a first come\, first se
 rve basis until capacity is reached.\nAbout the AI Tools Used in This Prog
 ram\nThis program includes activities that use agentic AI tools (listed be
 low) on library laptops.\nYou do not need to create your own account for t
 his program.\nParticipants are encouraged to follow the Resident’s guida
 nce throughout sessions\, which include best practices on using AI.\nWe en
 courage you to review the privacy statements of each tool prior to attendi
 ng this program:\nClaude Code\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy p
 olicy\nGoogle Antigravity\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy polic
 y\nGoogle AI Studio\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nInf
 ormation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is coll
 ected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, secti
 ons 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event
  and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how t
 he library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at
 \nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2
 G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e24aafa61002961b09b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
