SUMMARY:High Park Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T200000
UID:6a8f69d42dac6e003721f7f8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Come and join us on the second Wednesday of every month to dis
 cuss a variety of books.\nEveryone is welcome! Drop-in\, no registration r
 equired.\n2026 Reading List:\nJanuary 14:\nWhat I Mean to Say\n: Remaking 
 Conversation in Our Time by Ian Williams\nFebruary 11:\nFahrenheit 451\nby
  Ray Bradbury\nMarch 11:\nThe Remains of the Day\nby Kazuo Ishiguro\nApril
  8:\nOrbital\nby Samantha Harvey\nMay 13:\nThe Empusium\n: A Health Resort
  Horror Story by Olga Tokarczuk\nJune 10:\nTruth Telling\nSeven Conversati
 ons About Indigenous Life in Canada by Michelle Good\nJuly 8:\nThree Days 
 in June\nby Anne Tyler\nAugust 12:\nThe Woman in the Library\nby Sulari Ge
 ntill\nSeptember 9:\nValentine in Montreal\nby Heather O'Neill\nOctober 14
 :\nOn Tyranny: Twenty Lessons from the Twentieth Century\nby Timothy Snyde
 r\nNovember 11:\nFive Tuesdays in Winter\nby Lily King\nDecember 9: Best B
 ooks of the Year Discussion
LOCATION:High Park\, Community Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8f69d42dac6e003721f7f8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69de5e272866a5b4884877f1"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Short Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T193000
UID:69de5e272866a5b4884877f1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you interested in joining a book club but are short on tim
 e?  Then join our Short Book Club\, where we read titles that are 250 page
 s or less. Meetings are held on the second Wednesday of each month.\nOur O
 ctober book is\nCrying in H Mart\nby Michelle Zauner\nSummary: Zauner\, o
 f indie band Japanese Breakfast\, presents a memoir about growing up Korea
 n-American\, losing her mother\, and forging her own identity. She tells o
 f growing up the only Asian-American kid at her school in Eugene\, Oregon\
 ; of struggling with her mother's high expectations of her\; of a painful 
 adolescence\; of treasured months spent in her grandmother's tiny apartmen
 t in Seoul\, where she and her mother would bond over heaping plates of fo
 od. As she grew up\, her Koreanness began to feel ever more distant\, even
  as she found the life she wanted to live. Her mother's diagnosis of termi
 nal pancreatic cancer when Michelle was twenty-five forced a reckoning wit
 h her identity and brought her to reclaim the gifts of taste\, language\, 
 and history her mother had given her.\nNew members are most welcome! Pleas
 e inquire at the circulation desk if you would like a copy of the book.
LOCATION:Parliament Street\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69de5e272866a5b4884877f1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
