BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b6e72cbfe80d71a4733d"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Orhan Pamuk's The Museum of Innocence: Novel and Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T200000
UID:6a85b6e72cbfe80d71a4733d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Nobel Prize-winner Orhan Pamuk speaks about the creation of Is
 tanbul's Museum of Innocence.\nRenowned Turkish novelist and Nobel Prize-w
 inner Orhan Pamuk joins celebrated museum and cultural planning leader\, G
 ail Lord\, for an extraordinary conversation about one of the most singula
 r acts of imagination in contemporary literature and museology: the creati
 on of Istanbul's Museum of Innocence.\nConceived in tandem with his acclai
 med novel of the same name\, Pamuk's museum houses the fictional world of 
 his characters Kemal and Füsun in a real\, physical space — a small hou
 se in Istanbul filled with everyday objects\, each one tied to a moment in
  the novel. The result is a haunting blur between fiction and reality\, wh
 ere a love story lives on not only on the page\, but in glass display case
 s\, drawers\, and rooms visitors can walk through.\nHow does one curate a 
 museum for people who never existed? What does it mean to give physical fo
 rm to memory\, longing\, and melancholy — the very textures of a novel? 
 And what challenges arise when an author becomes a museum-maker\, translat
 ing narrative into space\, object\, and atmosphere?\nOrhan Pamuk sits down
  with Gail Lord\, co-founder of Lord Cultural Resources and one of the wor
 ld's leading museum planners\, to discuss the twin creation of the novel a
 nd the museum\, the ethereal act of turning fiction into physical experien
 ce\, and what he hoped visitors would feel wandering through the rooms of 
 a story. The conversation will also turn to Pamuk's evolving philosophy of
  museums — the ideas first laid out in his "Modest Manifesto for Museums
 " — exploring his belief in small\, intimate institutions that honor ind
 ividual and everyday stories over the grand\, monumental narratives so oft
 en privileged by national museums.\nThe discussion will also look ahead to
  Pamuk's second museum project\, currently in development\, in which he co
 ntinues to explore the relationship between storytelling\, memory\, and co
 llected objects. Building on the foundations laid by the Museum of Innocen
 ce\, this new endeavor promises to further test the boundaries between lit
 erature and lived space\, offering fresh insight into how narrative can be
  translated into an immersive\, tangible experience.\nQ&A and book signing
  to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nO
 rhan Pamuk\nGail Lord\nRead more:\nThe Museum of Innocence\nThe Innocence 
 of Objects\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\,
  Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors op
 en 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nT
 icket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest number
  of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm 
 or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar:\nBe
 er/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\n***\nThis event is pa
 rt of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and internationa
 l authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books and
  big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information
  on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public 
 Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will o
 nly be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. 
 Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the prog
 ram. Questions about how the library handles your information can be direc
 ted to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge St
 reet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b6e72cbfe80d71a4733d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
